# Feature: DOR1 on a SYMMETRIC bimolecular rule — both reactants are the same
#          molecule type binding on the same component, and one is tagged.
#
# STATUS:  CHARACTERIZATION TEST.  This model pins RM to NFsim on a point
#          where NFsim and BNG2 genuinely disagree; it is not evidence that
#          NFsim is right.  See issue #36.
#
#          The rule gives one physical event — molecules a and b binding —
#          two different rates depending on which one the tag lands on:
#          f(a) if a is tagged, f(b) if b is tagged.  The two engines
#          resolve that differently:
#
#            NFsim      SUMS the two labelings.  Effective rate f(a) + f(b).
#            BNG2 net   AVERAGES them.  It emits both orderings as separate
#                       reactions and applies symmetry_factor = 0.5 to each,
#                       giving (f(a) + f(b))/2.
#
#          Measured, 40 seeds / BNG2's own integrator, Afree at t = 10/20/30:
#            NFsim     33.5 / 17.9 / 13.0
#            RM        33.7 / 18.9 / 12.8      <- follows NFsim
#            BNG2 ODE  60.8 / 33.8 / 23.6      <- exactly 2x slower
#
#          RM follows NFsim because NFsim is RM's declared parity baseline,
#          not because the question is settled.  If issue #36 resolves the
#          other way, THIS MODEL'S REFERENCE DATA CHANGES — the DOR1
#          propensity machinery it rides on does not.  The oracle-clean
#          coverage of that machinery is nf_local_fcn_bimol, whose four arms
#          NFsim, BNG2 and RM all agree on.
#
#          Note this is a different disagreement from issue #33 (per-molecule
#          vs per-complex context multiplicity), which needs no local
#          function to reproduce and does not arise here — both reactants are
#          single molecules.
#
# Tests:   coverage of the code path a symmetric DOR1 rule takes, which is
#          not the plain homodimer path: it routes through the same-molecule
#          null-event rejection rather than the `same_components` ab_both
#          deflation.  The draw is rejected when both slots land on the same
#          molecule, which realizes the distinct ordered-pair sum exactly.
#          Untagged homodimer rules are unaffected and still take the 1/2
#          (see tests/cpp/homodimer_rate_test.cpp, which checks that against
#          the chemical master equation).
# Tier:    network-free.
# Expected: A(b) is consumed in pairs; m~1 molecules are twice as likely to
#          be drawn into the tagged slot as m~0 ones, so Afree collapses fast
#          and Amod stays pinned (the rule never changes m).
# invariant: conserved Atot = 400
# invariant: conserved Amod = 200

begin model
begin parameters
    kc   1.0e-3
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(b,m~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(b,m~0)   200
    A(b,m~1)   200
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  Afree   A(b)
    Molecules  Amod    A(m~1)
    Molecules  Atot    A()
    Molecules  Dim     A(b!1).A(b!1)
end observables

begin functions
    # 1 for an m~0 molecule, 2 for an m~1 one (molecule-level tag scope).
    lf(x) = kc*(1+Amod(x))
end functions

begin reaction rules
    R: A(b)%x + A(b) -> A(b!1)%x.A(b!1)   lf(x)
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>30,n_steps=>60})
end actions
